Thanks [~accountid:712020:3d2dfcb9-5b90-4b13-a4e2-9b7530e3d83f] - I think we now have enough information from JFL to proceed on this one. I summarise my thoughts below.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Are we LEGALLY able to share the requested information with JFL?*
{panel}

In short - *Probably yes* ✅ 

* The legal basis that JFL has cited (from the UK DPA) is not relevant. It does not compel Bolt to share this information in any way, nor give Bolt an explicit legal basis for sharing the data.
* _However_, we have enough information here to infer that JFL is asking for this information so that their client can bring civil proceedings against the driver of the third-party vehicle that collided with their client’s Bolt car. In the context of legal proceedings, Bolt may be able to share the requested information on the basis of _legitimate interest_ as a legal basis - regulators have often taken the position that companies should not thwart people’s rights to bring legal proceedings. As such, if push came to shove (which in any case it has not - see below), we could consider this approach.

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Are we PRACTICALLY able to share the requested information with JFL?*
{panel}

In short - *Probably not* ❌ 

* Based on the information provided, I have identified the relevant trip on our systems ([here|https://admin-panel.bolt.eu/ride-hailing-order/orders/order/591435617/1055?orderTryId=1377663584]). I have had a flick through the message threads / trip information and, unsurprisingly, we have not recorded the third-party’s licence plate. The only context in which we would have it is if either the driver or rider had specifically sent it to us - it looks like they have not. As such, we could not share this information even if we wanted to.

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Conclusion / Next Steps*
{panel}

# Check with someone from the UK Customer Support Team to see if either the driver or the rider of the trip did actually flag the third-party’s vehicle registration to us at any point (or if there is any reason why we otherwise have it on our systems). I doubt that this will be the case, but it is worth double-checking.
# If we do not have the information, then that is that. Respond to JFL saying that we have taken a look and unfortunately Bolt does not have third-party vehicle registrations on its systems - only the vehicle registrations of cars used to take Bolt trips.

